1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,153
Some oil platforms stand in water deeper

2
00:00:03,153 --> 00:00:04,887
than the Grand Canyon.

3
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:07,086
They are as heavy as a city

4
00:00:07,086 --> 00:00:09,777
block, they face waves taller than a

5
00:00:09,777 --> 00:00:12,393
house, and they must stay in almost

6
00:00:12,393 --> 00:00:14,934
exactly the same spot for decades,

7
00:00:14,934 --> 00:00:17,401
because a pipe connects them to a

8
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:19,046
hole in the sea floor.

9
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:21,128
If they drift too far,

10
00:00:21,128 --> 00:00:22,511
the pipe breaks.

11
00:00:22,691 --> 00:00:24,224
So how do they stay put?

12
00:00:24,404 --> 00:00:27,868
Today, ten parts of offshore platforms,

13
00:00:27,868 --> 00:00:30,000
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:33,401
The first platforms were built in shallow

15
00:00:33,401 --> 00:00:35,484
water, on simple steel legs,

16
00:00:35,484 --> 00:00:37,568
like a pier in the middle of

17
00:00:37,568 --> 00:00:38,163
the sea.

18
00:00:38,343 --> 00:00:40,671
But the oil kept moving deeper,

19
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:42,999
from tens of meters of water to

20
00:00:42,999 --> 00:00:45,402
hundreds, and then to thousands.

21
00:00:45,582 --> 00:00:46,715
At each step,

22
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:49,590
engineers had to invent a new way

23
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:52,553
to hold a giant structure still on

24
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:53,860
a moving ocean.

25
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:56,962
That is why offshore platforms come in

26
00:00:56,962 --> 00:00:58,730
so many strange shapes.

27
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:01,902
As always, every fact comes from a

28
00:01:01,902 --> 00:01:05,221
named source, official accident reports,

29
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:08,956
energy companies, and engineering references,

30
00:01:08,956 --> 00:01:11,445
all listed in the description.

31
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:14,147
Let's start with a platform that stands

32
00:01:14,147 --> 00:01:15,375
up on its own legs.

33
00:01:15,726 --> 00:01:18,430
Why do some oil rigs lift themselves

34
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:19,708
out of the water?

35
00:01:19,888 --> 00:01:21,989
They are called jack-ups.

36
00:01:22,169 --> 00:01:24,513
The rig floats to the site like

37
00:01:24,513 --> 00:01:27,235
a barge, with its long legs pointing

38
00:01:27,235 --> 00:01:28,067
to the sky.

39
00:01:28,247 --> 00:01:30,816
Then motors walk the legs down to

40
00:01:30,816 --> 00:01:31,906
the sea floor,

41
00:01:31,906 --> 00:01:34,786
and keep pushing until the whole hull

42
00:01:34,786 --> 00:01:36,420
rises out of the sea.

43
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,780
Now the rig stands on the bottom

44
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:39,666
like a table,

45
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:42,323
high enough that the biggest waves pass

46
00:01:42,323 --> 00:01:43,276
underneath it.

47
00:01:43,627 --> 00:01:45,861
At the bottom of each leg is

48
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:48,174
a big foot called a spud can,

49
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:50,728
shaped a bit like an upside down

50
00:01:50,728 --> 00:01:51,127
cone.

51
00:01:51,307 --> 00:01:54,538
Before drilling, the crew fills tanks with

52
00:01:54,538 --> 00:01:57,384
seawater to make the rig much heavier

53
00:01:57,384 --> 00:01:58,307
than normal.

54
00:01:58,487 --> 00:02:00,264
This is called preloading.

55
00:02:00,444 --> 00:02:02,891
It pushes the feet deep into the

56
00:02:02,891 --> 00:02:04,879
seabed and tests the soil.

57
00:02:05,059 --> 00:02:07,426
If the ground is going to give

58
00:02:07,426 --> 00:02:09,793
way, it is much better to find

59
00:02:09,793 --> 00:02:11,055
out now, slowly,

60
00:02:11,055 --> 00:02:12,791
than later in a storm.

61
00:02:13,141 --> 00:02:14,801
Jack-ups have a limit.

62
00:02:14,981 --> 00:02:17,382
Their legs can only be so long,

63
00:02:17,382 --> 00:02:20,558
so they work in relatively shallow water,

64
00:02:20,558 --> 00:02:23,657
typically up to around one hundred fifty

65
00:02:23,657 --> 00:02:24,199
meters.

66
00:02:24,379 --> 00:02:26,387
When the job is done,

67
00:02:26,387 --> 00:02:28,586
the legs climb back up,

68
00:02:28,586 --> 00:02:31,741
the rig floats again and is towed

69
00:02:31,741 --> 00:02:33,748
to the next location.

70
00:02:33,928 --> 00:02:36,273
A building that walks down to the

71
00:02:36,273 --> 00:02:38,617
sea floor and climbs back up when

72
00:02:38,617 --> 00:02:39,896
it wants to leave.

73
00:02:40,246 --> 00:02:43,565
Why do some floating platforms pull themselves

74
00:02:43,565 --> 00:02:45,586
down instead of floating up?

75
00:02:45,766 --> 00:02:48,383
A tension leg platform has a hull

76
00:02:48,383 --> 00:02:51,238
that wants to float much higher than

77
00:02:51,238 --> 00:02:51,873
it does.

78
00:02:52,053 --> 00:02:53,857
But it is tied to the sea

79
00:02:53,857 --> 00:02:57,104
floor by vertical steel tubes called tendons,

80
00:02:57,104 --> 00:02:58,764
pulled extremely tight.

81
00:02:58,944 --> 00:03:00,807
The buoyancy pulls up,

82
00:03:00,807 --> 00:03:02,670
the tendons pull down.

83
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:05,758
The platform can slide a little sideways

84
00:03:05,758 --> 00:03:08,084
in waves, but it barely moves up

85
00:03:08,084 --> 00:03:09,247
and down at all.

86
00:03:09,598 --> 00:03:12,488
That vertical stillness is the whole point.

87
00:03:12,668 --> 00:03:15,044
On a platform that does not bob,

88
00:03:15,044 --> 00:03:17,716
the wells can be controlled from the

89
00:03:17,716 --> 00:03:19,646
deck, almost like on land.

90
00:03:19,826 --> 00:03:23,131
The first tension leg platform to produce

91
00:03:23,131 --> 00:03:24,340
oil was Hutton,

92
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:26,355
in the British North Sea,

93
00:03:26,355 --> 00:03:28,290
in nineteen eighty-four.

94
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:30,849
Later ones in the Gulf of Mexico

95
00:03:30,849 --> 00:03:33,601
went into water more than a kilometer

96
00:03:33,601 --> 00:03:33,973
deep.

97
00:03:34,153 --> 00:03:37,383
And Heidrun, in the Norwegian Sea,

98
00:03:37,383 --> 00:03:39,377
is even more unusual.

99
00:03:39,557 --> 00:03:42,402
Its floating hull is made of concrete.

100
00:03:42,752 --> 00:03:44,949
The tendons are a bit like the

101
00:03:44,949 --> 00:03:46,415
strings of a guitar,

102
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:49,199
tight enough that they never go slack,

103
00:03:49,199 --> 00:03:51,250
even when a big wave passes.

104
00:03:51,430 --> 00:03:53,906
If a tendon ever went slack and

105
00:03:53,906 --> 00:03:55,902
then snapped tight again,

106
00:03:55,902 --> 00:03:57,978
the shock could damage it.

107
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:01,450
So engineers design the platform with so

108
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:04,825
much extra buoyancy that the tendons stay

109
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:07,376
tight in any storm they expect.

110
00:04:07,556 --> 00:04:09,356
Tension is the safety.

111
00:04:09,706 --> 00:04:12,182
Why do some platforms sit on one

112
00:04:12,182 --> 00:04:13,653
huge vertical tube?

113
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:15,240
They are called spars,

114
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:17,479
and they work like a fishing float.

115
00:04:17,659 --> 00:04:19,360
The tube is very long,

116
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:21,061
most of it underwater,

117
00:04:21,061 --> 00:04:22,918
and its bottom is heavy,

118
00:04:22,918 --> 00:04:25,470
so the center of gravity sits far

119
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:27,713
below the center of buoyancy.

120
00:04:27,893 --> 00:04:29,834
Like a float with a weight at

121
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:32,245
the bottom, it always wants to stand

122
00:04:32,245 --> 00:04:32,781
upright.

123
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:34,917
And because most of it is deep

124
00:04:34,917 --> 00:04:35,961
below the waves,

125
00:04:35,961 --> 00:04:38,243
the surface motion barely moves it.

126
00:04:38,593 --> 00:04:40,930
The first production spar,

127
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:44,255
Neptune, was installed in the Gulf of

128
00:04:44,255 --> 00:04:46,952
Mexico in nineteen ninety-six.

129
00:04:47,132 --> 00:04:49,045
One of the most famous,

130
00:04:49,045 --> 00:04:52,790
Perdido, floats over water about two thousand

131
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:55,369
four hundred fifty meters deep,

132
00:04:55,369 --> 00:04:57,616
around eight thousand feet.

133
00:04:57,796 --> 00:05:00,554
Its hull is about one hundred seventy

134
00:05:00,554 --> 00:05:03,686
meters long, taller than many skyscrapers,

135
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:05,773
and it was towed to the site

136
00:05:05,773 --> 00:05:07,116
lying on its side,

137
00:05:07,116 --> 00:05:09,278
then flooded to flip upright.

138
00:05:09,628 --> 00:05:11,831
A spar is held in place by

139
00:05:11,831 --> 00:05:15,305
mooring lines spread out like the strings

140
00:05:15,305 --> 00:05:16,152
of a tent.

141
00:05:16,332 --> 00:05:17,458
In deep water,

142
00:05:17,458 --> 00:05:20,112
steel chain would be too heavy to

143
00:05:20,112 --> 00:05:21,238
hang that far,

144
00:05:21,238 --> 00:05:23,570
so the middle of each line is

145
00:05:23,570 --> 00:05:25,902
often made of polyester rope,

146
00:05:25,902 --> 00:05:29,440
strong, flexible, and much lighter in water.

147
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,123
A platform in the deep ocean is

148
00:05:32,123 --> 00:05:34,950
often held by something closer to a

149
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:36,888
giant rope than a chain.

150
00:05:37,238 --> 00:05:40,332
Why doesn't this platform need any anchors

151
00:05:40,332 --> 00:05:40,848
at all?

152
00:05:41,028 --> 00:05:43,509
Because it is simply too heavy to

153
00:05:43,509 --> 00:05:43,884
move.

154
00:05:44,064 --> 00:05:46,755
It is a gravity-based structure,

155
00:05:46,755 --> 00:05:50,118
a giant made of reinforced concrete that

156
00:05:50,118 --> 00:05:52,473
sits on the sea floor and is

157
00:05:52,473 --> 00:05:54,827
held down by its own weight.

158
00:05:55,007 --> 00:05:57,625
The most famous is Troll A in

159
00:05:57,625 --> 00:05:58,258
Norway.

160
00:05:58,438 --> 00:06:01,381
Its total height is about four hundred

161
00:06:01,381 --> 00:06:05,255
seventy-two meters, and in nineteen ninety-five it

162
00:06:05,255 --> 00:06:06,882
was towed out to sea,

163
00:06:06,882 --> 00:06:10,213
becoming one of the tallest structures ever

164
00:06:10,213 --> 00:06:11,452
moved by humans.

165
00:06:11,803 --> 00:06:14,572
Troll A stands in about three hundred

166
00:06:14,572 --> 00:06:15,769
meters of water,

167
00:06:15,769 --> 00:06:17,940
so most of it is hidden below

168
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:18,838
the surface.

169
00:06:19,018 --> 00:06:22,830
Concrete giants like this were built standing

170
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:25,880
in deep, sheltered Norwegian fjords.

171
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:27,915
The base was built first,

172
00:06:27,915 --> 00:06:30,587
then flooded to sink lower and lower

173
00:06:30,587 --> 00:06:32,666
while the walls grew taller.

174
00:06:32,846 --> 00:06:35,880
When finished, the whole thing was floated

175
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:38,119
out using air in its big hollow

176
00:06:38,119 --> 00:06:40,937
cells, and then flooded again to settle

177
00:06:40,937 --> 00:06:41,948
on the seabed.

178
00:06:42,298 --> 00:06:46,088
In Canada, the Hibernia platform uses the

179
00:06:46,088 --> 00:06:48,768
same idea for another reason,

180
00:06:48,768 --> 00:06:49,600
icebergs.

181
00:06:49,780 --> 00:06:52,286
Its gravity base has an ice wall

182
00:06:52,286 --> 00:06:55,418
with teeth designed to resist the impact

183
00:06:55,418 --> 00:06:56,514
of an iceberg.

184
00:06:56,694 --> 00:06:58,489
It sits off Newfoundland,

185
00:06:58,489 --> 00:07:01,073
in the stretch of the North Atlantic

186
00:07:01,073 --> 00:07:02,580
called Iceberg Alley.

187
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:04,786
When you cannot move out of the

188
00:07:04,786 --> 00:07:06,290
way, you become a wall.

189
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:09,079
Why do floating rigs need so many

190
00:07:09,079 --> 00:07:09,670
anchors?

191
00:07:09,850 --> 00:07:13,389
A semi-submersible rig floats on giant pontoons

192
00:07:13,389 --> 00:07:14,970
deep under the water,

193
00:07:14,970 --> 00:07:17,605
with columns rising up to the deck.

194
00:07:17,785 --> 00:07:20,628
Because the pontoons are below the waves,

195
00:07:20,628 --> 00:07:22,778
it moves much less than a ship.

196
00:07:22,958 --> 00:07:24,523
But it still floats,

197
00:07:24,523 --> 00:07:26,480
so it is held in place by

198
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,454
eight, ten or more anchor lines spread

199
00:07:29,454 --> 00:07:30,237
around it.

200
00:07:30,417 --> 00:07:32,495
Each link of the chain can be

201
00:07:32,495 --> 00:07:34,573
the size of a person's torso.

202
00:07:34,924 --> 00:07:37,577
A floating rig is only as strong

203
00:07:37,577 --> 00:07:38,987
as its structure.

204
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:40,792
In nineteen eighty,

205
00:07:40,792 --> 00:07:43,614
the semi-submersible Alexander L.

206
00:07:43,794 --> 00:07:46,958
Kielland, used as a floating hotel for

207
00:07:46,958 --> 00:07:49,373
oil workers in the North Sea,

208
00:07:49,373 --> 00:07:51,038
capsized in a storm.

209
00:07:51,218 --> 00:07:53,366
One of its bracing tubes broke.

210
00:07:53,546 --> 00:07:56,749
The investigation found a fatigue crack that

211
00:07:56,749 --> 00:07:59,151
had started at a poorly made weld

212
00:07:59,151 --> 00:08:00,826
around a small fitting.

213
00:08:01,006 --> 00:08:03,548
One hundred twenty-three people died.

214
00:08:03,899 --> 00:08:07,394
That disaster changed the rules for offshore

215
00:08:07,394 --> 00:08:10,890
structures, with more redundancy so that one

216
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:13,670
broken member cannot capsize a rig,

217
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:16,530
and better lifeboats and evacuation.

218
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:20,307
Offshore engineering is full of lessons like

219
00:08:20,307 --> 00:08:20,716
this.

220
00:08:20,896 --> 00:08:23,762
A platform is designed to survive the

221
00:08:23,762 --> 00:08:25,157
hundred-year wave.

222
00:08:25,337 --> 00:08:28,294
The real danger is often a small

223
00:08:28,294 --> 00:08:29,864
crack nobody saw.

224
00:08:30,215 --> 00:08:33,265
Why do some drilling ships have propellers

225
00:08:33,265 --> 00:08:35,298
pointing in every direction,

226
00:08:35,298 --> 00:08:37,332
when they never go anywhere?

227
00:08:37,512 --> 00:08:39,915
Because in very deep water,

228
00:08:39,915 --> 00:08:42,318
anchors become impractical.

229
00:08:42,498 --> 00:08:45,714
So these ships use dynamic positioning.

230
00:08:45,894 --> 00:08:50,591
Satellite navigation, motion sensors and wind sensors

231
00:08:50,591 --> 00:08:52,009
feed a computer,

232
00:08:52,009 --> 00:08:56,085
which constantly adjusts six or more thrusters

233
00:08:56,085 --> 00:08:58,301
to push against the wind,

234
00:08:58,301 --> 00:08:59,896
waves and current.

235
00:09:00,076 --> 00:09:02,446
The ship can hold its position over

236
00:09:02,446 --> 00:09:04,275
a well within a few meters,

237
00:09:04,275 --> 00:09:06,171
without touching the bottom.

238
00:09:06,521 --> 00:09:08,587
The idea started early.

239
00:09:08,767 --> 00:09:10,512
In nineteen sixty-one,

240
00:09:10,512 --> 00:09:13,763
the drilling vessel CUSS One drilled into

241
00:09:13,763 --> 00:09:16,618
the sea floor off Mexico for Project

242
00:09:16,618 --> 00:09:21,059
Mohole, holding position with steerable propellers while

243
00:09:21,059 --> 00:09:23,756
operators watched buoys and radar.

244
00:09:23,936 --> 00:09:27,010
Today, the system is fully automated and

245
00:09:27,010 --> 00:09:29,163
has backups for its backups,

246
00:09:29,163 --> 00:09:32,545
because a drillship that drifts off position

247
00:09:32,545 --> 00:09:34,928
while connected to a well is an

248
00:09:34,928 --> 00:09:35,697
emergency.

249
00:09:36,047 --> 00:09:39,008
Dynamic positioning is now used by many

250
00:09:39,008 --> 00:09:40,147
kinds of ships,

251
00:09:40,147 --> 00:09:43,260
from cable layers to cruise ships waiting

252
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:45,842
near a port where anchoring is not

253
00:09:45,842 --> 00:09:46,449
allowed.

254
00:09:46,629 --> 00:09:48,440
The ship is not parked.

255
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:51,118
It is driving very hard to stay

256
00:09:51,118 --> 00:09:52,730
exactly where it is.

257
00:09:52,910 --> 00:09:55,810
Standing still, it turns out,

258
00:09:55,810 --> 00:09:57,810
burns a lot of fuel.

259
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:00,035
Why is there a big hole in

260
00:10:00,035 --> 00:10:02,200
the middle of a drilling ship?

261
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:04,214
It is called the moonpool,

262
00:10:04,214 --> 00:10:06,823
an opening straight through the hull,

263
00:10:06,823 --> 00:10:08,940
from the deck down to the sea.

264
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:10,233
The drill pipe,

265
00:10:10,233 --> 00:10:12,833
the riser and the equipment go down

266
00:10:12,833 --> 00:10:15,729
through it, protected from the waves at

267
00:10:15,729 --> 00:10:17,363
the sides of the ship.

268
00:10:17,543 --> 00:10:20,709
Supposedly the name comes from the moonlight

269
00:10:20,709 --> 00:10:23,299
reflecting on the water inside it at

270
00:10:23,299 --> 00:10:23,731
night.

271
00:10:24,081 --> 00:10:25,810
But there is another problem.

272
00:10:25,990 --> 00:10:28,591
The ship rises and falls with the

273
00:10:28,591 --> 00:10:31,349
waves, sometimes by several meters,

274
00:10:31,349 --> 00:10:34,501
while the drill bit is kilometers below,

275
00:10:34,501 --> 00:10:37,022
trying to press on the rock with

276
00:10:37,022 --> 00:10:38,204
a steady force.

277
00:10:38,384 --> 00:10:40,737
So the drill string hangs from a

278
00:10:40,737 --> 00:10:44,120
heave compensator, a system of giant cylinders

279
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:46,840
and pressurized gas that works like a

280
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:49,781
shock absorber, letting the ship move up

281
00:10:49,781 --> 00:10:52,502
and down while the pipe stays steady.

282
00:10:52,852 --> 00:10:55,388
The pipe that connects the ship to

283
00:10:55,388 --> 00:10:57,551
the well is called the riser,

284
00:10:57,551 --> 00:11:00,013
and in ultra-deep water it can be

285
00:11:00,013 --> 00:11:02,400
more than three kilometers long,

286
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:04,936
hanging from the ship like a giant

287
00:11:04,936 --> 00:11:05,832
steel straw.

288
00:11:06,012 --> 00:11:07,651
Currents push on it,

289
00:11:07,651 --> 00:11:09,126
the ship pulls it,

290
00:11:09,126 --> 00:11:11,584
and it must not bend too much.

291
00:11:11,764 --> 00:11:13,999
That is the real reason a rig

292
00:11:13,999 --> 00:11:15,387
cannot float away.

293
00:11:15,567 --> 00:11:18,014
It is attached to the sea floor

294
00:11:18,014 --> 00:11:18,804
by a long,

295
00:11:18,804 --> 00:11:19,909
fragile straw.

296
00:11:20,259 --> 00:11:22,887
Why does every offshore well have a

297
00:11:22,887 --> 00:11:26,342
building-sized valve sitting on the sea floor?

298
00:11:26,522 --> 00:11:28,333
It is the blowout preventer.

299
00:11:28,513 --> 00:11:30,828
If gas or oil rushes up the

300
00:11:30,828 --> 00:11:34,342
well uncontrolled, the crew can close it.

301
00:11:34,522 --> 00:11:37,365
Some of its parts squeeze rubber around

302
00:11:37,365 --> 00:11:39,406
the pipe, and the strongest,

303
00:11:39,406 --> 00:11:40,718
called shear rams,

304
00:11:40,718 --> 00:11:43,634
are designed to cut straight through the

305
00:11:43,634 --> 00:11:46,550
drill pipe and seal the well completely.

306
00:11:46,730 --> 00:11:49,226
It is the last line of defense.

307
00:11:49,576 --> 00:11:51,283
On April twentieth,

308
00:11:51,283 --> 00:11:54,879
twenty ten, the Deepwater Horizon rig in

309
00:11:54,879 --> 00:11:58,295
the Gulf of Mexico suffered a blowout.

310
00:11:58,475 --> 00:12:01,056
Gas reached the rig and exploded,

311
00:12:01,056 --> 00:12:02,933
and eleven workers died.

312
00:12:03,113 --> 00:12:06,800
The blowout preventer was activated but failed

313
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:08,163
to seal the well.

314
00:12:08,343 --> 00:12:11,449
Investigators found that the drill pipe had

315
00:12:11,449 --> 00:12:13,110
been pushed off center,

316
00:12:13,110 --> 00:12:15,348
so the shear rams could not cut

317
00:12:15,348 --> 00:12:16,865
and seal it properly.

318
00:12:17,045 --> 00:12:20,387
Oil flowed into the Gulf for eighty-seven

319
00:12:20,387 --> 00:12:20,795
days.

320
00:12:21,145 --> 00:12:22,604
After the disaster,

321
00:12:22,604 --> 00:12:26,214
rules for blowout preventers became stricter in

322
00:12:26,214 --> 00:12:27,596
the United States,

323
00:12:27,596 --> 00:12:31,283
with more testing and requirements for equipment

324
00:12:31,283 --> 00:12:34,125
that can cut pipe in more conditions.

325
00:12:34,305 --> 00:12:37,321
The giant valve is a perfect example

326
00:12:37,321 --> 00:12:39,332
of offshore engineering.

327
00:12:39,512 --> 00:12:42,029
It works silently for years at the

328
00:12:42,029 --> 00:12:43,362
bottom of the sea,

329
00:12:43,362 --> 00:12:45,805
and it must work perfectly on the

330
00:12:45,805 --> 00:12:47,360
one day it is needed.

331
00:12:47,710 --> 00:12:50,099
Why do many oil platforms have a

332
00:12:50,099 --> 00:12:52,488
long arm with a flame burning at

333
00:12:52,488 --> 00:12:53,085
the end?

334
00:12:53,265 --> 00:12:54,403
It is the flare.

335
00:12:54,583 --> 00:12:57,172
The most important reason is safety.

336
00:12:57,352 --> 00:12:59,977
If pressure builds up somewhere in the

337
00:12:59,977 --> 00:13:02,532
process, or if there is an emergency,

338
00:13:02,532 --> 00:13:04,190
gas has to go somewhere.

339
00:13:04,370 --> 00:13:07,778
Releasing it unburned near a platform full

340
00:13:07,778 --> 00:13:10,781
of people and engines could create an

341
00:13:10,781 --> 00:13:12,079
explosive cloud.

342
00:13:12,259 --> 00:13:13,738
So it is sent to the end

343
00:13:13,738 --> 00:13:14,477
of the boom,

344
00:13:14,477 --> 00:13:15,587
far from the deck,

345
00:13:15,587 --> 00:13:16,265
and burned.

346
00:13:16,615 --> 00:13:19,088
The boom sticks out far from the

347
00:13:19,088 --> 00:13:21,793
platform, and it is often placed so

348
00:13:21,793 --> 00:13:24,576
that the wind usually blows the heat

349
00:13:24,576 --> 00:13:24,962
away.

350
00:13:25,142 --> 00:13:26,466
In an emergency,

351
00:13:26,466 --> 00:13:29,445
a platform can send large amounts of

352
00:13:29,445 --> 00:13:31,762
gas to the flare in minutes,

353
00:13:31,762 --> 00:13:36,230
called blowdown, to empty dangerous equipment quickly.

354
00:13:36,410 --> 00:13:39,487
A small pilot flame often burns all

355
00:13:39,487 --> 00:13:42,301
the time, so the flare is always

356
00:13:42,301 --> 00:13:43,619
ready to light.

357
00:13:43,970 --> 00:13:44,900
In the past,

358
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,156
many fields also flared gas simply because

359
00:13:48,156 --> 00:13:50,715
there was no pipeline to sell it.

360
00:13:50,895 --> 00:13:53,243
That is wasteful and polluting,

361
00:13:53,243 --> 00:13:56,728
and today many countries limit routine flaring

362
00:13:56,728 --> 00:13:59,531
and push companies to capture the gas

363
00:13:59,531 --> 00:14:00,137
instead.

364
00:14:00,317 --> 00:14:02,383
But the safety flare stays.

365
00:14:02,563 --> 00:14:03,627
On a platform,

366
00:14:03,627 --> 00:14:06,135
the most dangerous gas is the gas

367
00:14:06,135 --> 00:14:07,579
with nowhere to go.

368
00:14:07,929 --> 00:14:11,033
Why are some lifeboats on oil platforms

369
00:14:11,033 --> 00:14:13,023
pointing down at the sea,

370
00:14:13,023 --> 00:14:15,649
like a ride at an amusement park?

371
00:14:15,829 --> 00:14:18,128
They are free-fall lifeboats.

372
00:14:18,308 --> 00:14:21,650
Instead of being lowered slowly by ropes,

373
00:14:21,650 --> 00:14:24,095
they slide off a ramp and drop

374
00:14:24,095 --> 00:14:26,052
straight into the water,

375
00:14:26,052 --> 00:14:29,394
sometimes from more than twenty or thirty

376
00:14:29,394 --> 00:14:29,964
meters.

377
00:14:30,144 --> 00:14:32,324
They hit the water nose first,

378
00:14:32,324 --> 00:14:35,013
dive, and come up already moving away

379
00:14:35,013 --> 00:14:36,321
from the platform.

380
00:14:36,671 --> 00:14:38,644
Why drop people from that height?

381
00:14:38,824 --> 00:14:42,340
Because on a burning or capsizing platform,

382
00:14:42,340 --> 00:14:44,629
slow lowering can be deadly.

383
00:14:44,809 --> 00:14:45,904
Ropes can jam,

384
00:14:45,904 --> 00:14:48,876
the boat can swing into the structure,

385
00:14:48,876 --> 00:14:51,535
and waves can smash it against the

386
00:14:51,535 --> 00:14:51,926
legs.

387
00:14:52,106 --> 00:14:55,043
A free-fall boat leaves in seconds and

388
00:14:55,043 --> 00:14:57,052
gets away from the danger.

389
00:14:57,232 --> 00:15:00,522
The seats face backward with strong harnesses,

390
00:15:00,522 --> 00:15:03,025
so the crew can survive the impact.

391
00:15:03,376 --> 00:15:05,511
The North Sea learned this the hard

392
00:15:05,511 --> 00:15:05,756
way.

393
00:15:05,936 --> 00:15:09,044
After the Alexander Kielland disaster,

394
00:15:09,044 --> 00:15:12,888
and later Piper Alpha in nineteen eighty-eight,

395
00:15:12,888 --> 00:15:15,505
where an explosion and fire on a

396
00:15:15,505 --> 00:15:19,349
platform killed one hundred sixty-seven people,

397
00:15:19,349 --> 00:15:22,375
offshore safety rules changed deeply.

398
00:15:22,555 --> 00:15:25,699
Free-fall lifeboats became common,

399
00:15:25,699 --> 00:15:27,640
especially in Norway.

400
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:30,231
A boat that falls on purpose is

401
00:15:30,231 --> 00:15:32,798
safer than one that might fall by

402
00:15:32,798 --> 00:15:33,498
accident.

403
00:15:33,848 --> 00:15:35,241
So here are the ten.

404
00:15:35,421 --> 00:15:37,520
Legs that walk down to the sea

405
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:37,940
floor.

406
00:15:38,120 --> 00:15:40,895
Cables that pull a floating hull down.

407
00:15:41,075 --> 00:15:42,944
A giant fishing float,

408
00:15:42,944 --> 00:15:45,747
and a concrete tower too heavy to

409
00:15:45,747 --> 00:15:46,172
move.

410
00:15:46,352 --> 00:15:49,521
Anchor lines, and propellers that drive just

411
00:15:49,521 --> 00:15:50,601
to stand still.

412
00:15:50,781 --> 00:15:52,977
A hole in the middle of a

413
00:15:52,977 --> 00:15:55,700
ship, a valve on the sea floor,

414
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:57,632
a flame on a long arm,

415
00:15:57,632 --> 00:16:00,882
and a lifeboat that falls on purpose.

416
00:16:01,232 --> 00:16:04,011
An oil platform does not stay in

417
00:16:04,011 --> 00:16:05,574
place by accident.

418
00:16:05,754 --> 00:16:07,705
Every shape fights the sea in a

419
00:16:07,705 --> 00:16:08,587
different way.

420
00:16:08,767 --> 00:16:11,714
If you want more machines explained part

421
00:16:11,714 --> 00:16:14,513
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

422
00:16:14,693 --> 00:16:16,701
And tell us in the comments which

423
00:16:16,701 --> 00:16:18,708
machine you want us to take apart

424
00:16:18,708 --> 00:16:19,012
next.
